## 6. Fit-quality inspection

Visual inspection was completed across the main SPM DCM result panels.

| Plot | Interpretation | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| ERPs (mode) | Model reproduced the main Standard and Pitch Deviant response profiles across the 8 spatial modes. Modes 1, 3, 5 and 6 showed especially good agreement; modes 2 and 4 were less exact but still plausible. | Acceptable |
| ERPs (sources) | Source responses were structured across bilateral A1, STG and IFG. Pitch Deviant responses were generally larger than Standard responses, especially in auditory and inferior frontal sources. | Acceptable |
| Coupling (A) | Baseline extrinsic connectivity followed the intended A1-STG-IFG hierarchy and did not show diffuse or unstable estimates. | Acceptable |
| Coupling (B) | Pitch Deviant modulation was interpretable, including A1 intrinsic modulation and STG/IFG fronto-temporal modulation. | Acceptable |
| Coupling (C) | Auditory input was estimated in bilateral A1 only: left A1 = 0.74, right A1 = 0.64, with posterior probabilities of 0.99 and 1.00. | Good |
| Trial-specific effects | Pitch Deviant showed reduced A1 intrinsic/self effects and increased STG/IFG fronto-temporal effects, especially in the left hemisphere. | Acceptable |
| Input | Single transient auditory input function peaked early after stimulus onset and returned toward zero. | Acceptable |
| Response | Observed adjusted and predicted responses matched well for both conditions. | Acceptable |
| Response (image) | Predicted channel-time images resembled observed images for both conditions. | Acceptable |
| Scalp maps | Predicted topographies closely matched observed topographies at representative time points for both conditions. | Good |
| Data | The observed data showed clear structured evoked responses, with stronger Pitch Deviant response than Standard. | Acceptable |

